.TH AGENT-SANDBOX-EXEC 1 "2026-07-01" "agent-sandbox-exec 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agent-sandbox-exec \- run a command inside the BPF-LSM agent sandbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent-sandbox-exec
.RB [ -h ]
.RB [ -V ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agent-sandbox-exec
runs
.I command
(and its arguments) inside the agent sandbox: the process and every process it
spawns is migrated into a per-uid agent cgroup
.RI ( /sys/fs/cgroup/agent-sandbox-exec/uid-<uid> ),
where a BPF-LSM program on the
.I file_open
hook blocks opens of the files listed in the denylist, returning
.BR EPERM .
.PP
The effective denylist for a uid is the union of the root-controlled base list
.RI ( /etc/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist )
and that uid's home list
.RI ( ~/.config/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist ).
A user-controlled home list is safe because deny entries are scoped to the
user's own cgroup: they can only block opens inside that user's own sandbox.
.PP
This defeats read-via-delegation \(em
.BR "cat secret | \&..." ,
hardlinks to a secret, and
.B "cp secret"
are all blocked, because the source
.B open
is denied.
.PP
No mount or user namespace is created, so the command sees the real environment
\(em real mount table, real
.I /dev
ownership, real supplementary groups, real
.I /proc
\(em and can still troubleshoot kernel and system issues accurately.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The command ran and exited 0.
.TP
.B 2
The sandbox was not ready, migration timed out (is
.B agent-sandbox-execd
running?), or no command was given. The command was
.I not
executed.
.TP
.I *
Otherwise the command's own exit status.
.SH "FAIL-CLOSED BEHAVIOUR"
Fail-closed is unconditional. If
.B agent-sandbox-execd
is not running, the agent cgroup is absent, or migration does not complete, the
command is
.I not
executed (exit 2). There is no escape-hatch environment variable: to run a
command without the sandbox, invoke it directly rather than through this
wrapper.
.PP
A uid's denylist is re-read from disk on every launch and diff-applied to that
uid's cgroup, so denylist changes (base or home) take effect on the user's next
launch \(em no daemon restart needed. A restart
.RB ( "systemctl restart agent-sandbox-execd" )
only refreshes a uid that has a running sandbox but no new launch.
.SH PROTOCOL
The wrapper cannot migrate the process itself \(em
.I cgroup.procs
is root-owned. It performs a one-file handshake with
.B agent-sandbox-execd
(the root daemon) over
.IR /run/agent-sandbox-exec/req :
.IP 1. 4
The wrapper creates an empty request file named after its own PID in that
directory (a root-owned, world-writable, sticky directory). The filename \(em
the PID \(em is the payload: it is the process the daemon must migrate.
.IP 2. 4
.B agent-sandbox-execd
watches the directory with
.BR inotify (7)
and, for each request, reads the target's real uid from
.IR /proc/<pid>/status .
It rejects root and requires the request file's owner
.RI ( st_uid )
to equal that real uid. This closes a pid-reuse race: if the requester pid
died and its number was reused by a different uid, the file's owner will not
match
.IR /proc .
A user can therefore migrate only their own processes.
.IP 3. 4
The daemon re-reads that uid's denylist (base \(cu home) from disk and
diff-applies it to the uid's cgroup, then migrates the pid into
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/agent-sandbox-exec/uid-<uid>/cgroup.procs .
The same request that asks for migration also reloads the denylist (the
ping-driven reload), so there is no separate reload channel and no inotify
watch on the denylists.
.IP 4. 4
The daemon removes the request file only after a verified successful
migration. Its disappearance is the wrapper's success signal: the wrapper
polls for it (up to ~1.5 s). If the file remains, migration failed or timed
out and the wrapper fails closed (exit 2; the command is not executed).
.PP
The daemon does not trust the wrapper. Identity and authorization are
re-derived from
.I /proc
and the request file's owner; denial is enforced by the kernel BPF-LSM hook,
not reported by the wrapper. A user can only migrate their own processes,
and deny entries are scoped to that user's own cgroup.
.PP
The hook returns
.B EPERM
("Operation not permitted"), the conventional errno for a security-policy
denial. It is distinct from
.BR EACCES ,
which the kernel's own mode-bit check returns before the hook fires, so a
denied open is unambiguously the denylist rather than an ordinary permissions
error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist
Root-controlled base denylist, applied to every sandboxed uid \(em see
.BR agent-sandbox-exec-denylist (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist
Per-user home denylist (NFS-safe); unioned with the base list for that uid.
.TP
.I /run/agent-sandbox-exec/req
Root-owned, world-writable, sticky directory holding one-shot migration
request files named by the launcher's PID \(em see
.B PROTOCOL
above.
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup/agent-sandbox-exec
Parent cgroup (root-owned, not delegated); per-uid children
.I uid-<uid>
hold the sandboxed processes.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR agent-sandbox-exec-denylist (5)